  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "The noun record is stressed on the first syllable, REC-ord; the verb record is stressed on the second, re-CORD. You keep a record of something and hold, set, or break a record for something. In the music sense the word now usually means a vinyl disc; a set of songs sold together is more often called an album.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "She made a new record in the race.",
      "correct": "She set a new record in the race.",
      "note": "The verbs for a best result are set, break, and beat; make a record means produce a music recording."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "The written-information sense, the best-ever sense, and the music sense are usually three different words in other languages, and only the first two are connected in meaning. Translate each sense separately.",
    "grammar": "Countable in every sense. Before another noun, record means the best or highest ever: record profits, a record number. Keep a record of something; hold, set, or break a record for something.",
    "culture": "A criminal record can make it hard to get some jobs, which is why people speak of a clean record. The phrases on the record and off the record come from journalism and are used whenever someone speaks to a reporter.",
    "false_friends": "In some languages a word that looks like record means a memory or a souvenir, and in others it has only the sports sense. English record is never a memory; its first sense is written information.",
    "pronunciation": "The noun has the stress on the first syllable, REC-ord, with a weak vowel in the second syllable in American English. The verb record has the stress on the second syllable, re-CORD. Keeping these two apart is the main trap with this word."
  },
